- "D."

269
01:07:13.322 --> 01:07:14.845
"E."

270
01:07:16.625 --> 01:07:18.414
"F."

271
01:07:18.494 --> 01:07:20.984
- "F."
- Mm-hmm.

272
01:07:21.764 --> 01:07:23.331
"G."

273
01:07:25.200 --> 01:07:27.589
- "G."
- Mm-hmm.

274
01:07:27.669 --> 01:07:30.271
Mm... "H."

275
01:07:32.607 --> 01:07:34.175
"I."

276
01:07:35.877 --> 01:07:37.400
"J."

277
01:07:37.580 --> 01:07:38.935
"J."

278
01:07:39.015 --> 01:07:40.545
Mm...

279
01:07:42.317 --> 01:07:43.874
"U."

280
01:07:45.054 --> 01:07:47.443
Um... "V."

281
01:07:47.523 --> 01:07:49.612
"V." "W"?

282
01:07:49.692 --> 01:07:51.214
"W."

283
01:07:51.844 --> 01:07:53.433
"X."

284
01:07:54.363 --> 01:07:55.885
"Y."

285
01:07:56.165 --> 01:07:57.698
"Z."

286
01:07:59.133 --> 01:08:00.990
Mm-hmm.

287
01:08:01.470 --> 01:08:03.025
Good.

288
01:08:03.105 --> 01:08:05.710
Oh! I almost forgot.

289
01:08:12.380 --> 01:08:13.948
Huh.

290
01:08:14.883 --> 01:08:17.273
Hmm. Remember her?

291
01:08:17.353 --> 01:08:18.740
Mm.

292
01:08:20.155 --> 01:08:22.478
My mom. <i>Mi madre.</i>

293
01:08:23.058 --> 01:08:24.680
She works a lot.

294
01:08:24.760 --> 01:08:28.217
We live in...

295
01:08:28.697 --> 01:08:30.264
...Queens.

296
01:08:33.134 --> 01:08:34.256
Queens?

297
01:08:34.336 --> 01:08:36.604
- Mm-hmm.
- You don't live in New York?

298
01:08:37.939 --> 01:08:40.808
Queens is in New York.

299
01:08:43.178 --> 01:08:44.768
What?

300
01:08:45.748 --> 01:08:48.639
- Forget it.
- Oh. And this.

301
01:08:50.519 --> 01:08:53.721
- Whoa.
- I found it in my mom's room.

302
01:08:55.507 --> 01:08:59.145
It's from here. Except a long time ago.

303
01:09:00.896 --> 01:09:05.333
It's where I found
the bookmark from Kincaid's.

304
01:09:07.068 --> 01:09:08.903
It's why I'm here.

305
01:09:14.443 --> 01:09:16.277
Hey, you okay?

306
01:09:17.112 --> 01:09:20.715
Sorry, I'm just really tired.

307
01:09:24.953 --> 01:09:27.410
Well, take a nap.

308
01:09:28.140 --> 01:09:29.641
Okay.

309
01:09:36.998 --> 01:09:38.533
Hmm?

310
01:09:43.005 --> 01:09:44.539
Ooh.

311
01:09:48.577 --> 01:09:51.413
- Um, I'll be back later.
- Thanks, Jamie.

312
01:11:50.364 --> 01:11:52.488
- Ben! Ben!
- Ow.

313
01:11:52.568 --> 01:11:56.526
Wake up! Come on, I told my father
I was staying over at a friend's house...

314
01:11:56.606 --> 01:11:59.061
- ...so we have the museum to ourselves!
- I can't hear you.

315
01:11:59.141 --> 01:12:01.995
Follow me, come on, come on!

316
01:12:31.940 --> 01:12:33.975
Shh. Come on.

317
01:12:55.496 --> 01:12:57.087
Wow.

318
01:12:57.867 --> 01:13:00.490
Hey, look. See?

319
01:13:01.370 --> 01:13:03.203
The light goes through.

320
01:13:10.311 --> 01:13:11.664
Neat.

321
01:13:20.438 --> 01:13:22.120
This is cool.

322
01:13:36.804 --> 01:13:38.227
- Hey.
- Hmm?

323
01:13:38.307 --> 01:13:40.173
Check this out.

324
01:13:42.277 --> 01:13:43.778
Ben.

325
01:13:48.449 --> 01:13:50.707
- Huh?
- Ben.

326
01:13:51.287 --> 01:13:53.142
Come on.

327
01:14:15.443 --> 01:14:18.267
Jamie, what is all this stuff?

328
01:14:18.347 --> 01:14:19.802
Shh.

329
01:14:20.082 --> 01:14:22.171
Secrets.

330
01:14:23.451 --> 01:14:25.153
Come on.

331
01:15:07.128 --> 01:15:08.450
- <i>Open it!</i>
- <i>What?</i>

332
01:15:08.530 --> 01:15:11.566
Come on, the file. Open it up.

333
01:15:14.969 --> 01:15:17.237
- The wolves.
- Whoa.

334
01:15:19.373 --> 01:15:24.035
- That is so cool.
- "February '65."

335
01:15:24.964 --> 01:15:26.998
That's the year before I was born.

336
01:15:34.722 --> 01:15:36.445
Whoa.

337
01:15:36.525 --> 01:15:37.646
Whoa.

338
01:15:37.726 --> 01:15:40.527
Yeah. Wow.

339
01:15:43.221 --> 01:15:45.678
- Aurora.
- Nice.

340
01:15:45.758 --> 01:15:47.357
Hmm.

341
01:15:47.937 --> 01:15:49.403
Look at this one.

342
01:15:54.108 --> 01:15:57.711
- What? What is it?
- It's my house.

343
01:16:13.928 --> 01:16:16.551
"Miss Elaine Wilson."

344
01:16:16.631 --> 01:16:19.020
That's my mom.

345
01:16:19.100 --> 01:16:23.458
"Dear Miss Wilson, I will be spending
a few months in Gunflint Lake...

346
01:16:23.538 --> 01:16:26.428
...researching a new diorama for the museum.

347
01:16:26.508 --> 01:16:30.666
As the town librarian, I hope
you can help me with this research.

348
01:16:30.746 --> 01:16:33.370
Please contact me at the address above.

349
01:16:34.650 --> 01:16:36.505
Thank you very much...

350
01:16:36.985 --> 01:16:38.799
...Daniel Lobel.

351
01:16:41.823 --> 01:16:43.658
Daniel Lobel.

352
01:16:46.935 --> 01:16:48.810
This has to be my dad.

353
01:16:50.899 --> 01:16:53.634
Your father, maybe he still remembers him.

354
01:16:54.502 --> 01:16:58.572
- You have to ask him in the morning.
- Yeah. Yeah, I will.

355
01:17:16.524 --> 01:17:20.723
"I've... never... shown...

356
01:17:20.803 --> 01:17:23.512
"...anyone... the museum...

357
01:17:25.150 --> 01:17:26.655
...before."

358
01:17:26.735 --> 01:17:30.993
- Hmm. Hmm.
- But I like it here.

359
01:17:31.073 --> 01:17:33.474
I don't have a room at my dad's place.

360
01:17:35.826 --> 01:17:39.464
Oh, hold on. I think you'll like this.

361
01:17:48.122 --> 01:17:49.623
Oh.

362
01:17:51.260 --> 01:17:53.094
I forgot.

363
01:17:54.796 --> 01:17:57.252
It's okay, keep it on.

364
01:17:57.332 --> 01:17:59.400
My mom had one like this.

365
01:18:01.302 --> 01:18:04.693
- I can feel the vibrations.
- <i>I</i>

366
01:18:04.773 --> 01:18:07.930
<i>Don't want to know your name</i>

367
01:18:08.010 --> 01:18:11.700
<i>'Cause you don't look the same</i>

368
01:18:11.780 --> 01:18:16.271
- "F." "R."
- <i>The way you did before</i>

369
01:18:16.351 --> 01:18:19.040
- "I."
- <i>Okay, okay</i>

370
01:18:19.120 --> 01:18:21.910
- <i>You think you got a pretty face</i>
- "E."

371
01:18:21.990 --> 01:18:24.647
- "N."
- <i>But the rest of you is out of place</i>

372
01:18:24.727 --> 01:18:26.682
"D." "Friend." Cool.

373
01:18:26.762 --> 01:18:30.219
<i>You looked all right before</i>

374
01:18:32.367 --> 01:18:34.956
<i>Fox on the run</i>

375
01:18:35.036 --> 01:18:39.728
<i>You scream and everybody comes a-running</i>

376
01:18:39.808 --> 01:18:42.373
<i>Take a run and hide...</i>

377
01:22:02.375 --> 01:22:04.512
Ben, Ben.

378
01:22:04.592 --> 01:22:06.142
Ben.

379
01:22:06.522 --> 01:22:09.375
Wake up! Come on.

380
01:22:10.185 --> 01:22:11.507
Come on.

381
01:22:11.587 --> 01:22:13.821
- What?
- Look.

382
01:22:18.192 --> 01:22:20.015
It's the same.

383
01:22:20.395 --> 01:22:22.371
It's the same.

384
01:22:22.881 --> 01:22:25.321
We're in the Cabinet of Wonders.

385
01:22:26.601 --> 01:22:28.435
How did this happen?

386
01:22:29.704 --> 01:22:31.939
Why did my father have this book?

387
01:22:35.643 --> 01:22:38.646
Why was there a picture
of my house in that file?

388
01:22:41.282 --> 01:22:43.083
What's going on?

389
01:22:44.685 --> 01:22:46.220
What?

390
01:22:49.590 --> 01:22:51.125
What?

391
01:22:58.835 --> 01:23:00.774
The bookstore? It's gone.

392
01:23:06.740 --> 01:23:08.830
"I tried to tell you.

393
01:23:08.910 --> 01:23:10.733
"It just moved.

394
01:23:11.913 --> 01:23:13.835
"I didn't know...

395
01:23:13.915 --> 01:23:16.550
...you were deaf yet."

396
01:23:18.786 --> 01:23:20.587
What do you mean?

397
01:23:22.189 --> 01:23:24.212
You knew I was trying to find my father.

398
01:23:24.292 --> 01:23:27.160
That's why I went to Kincaid's
in the first place.

399
01:23:28.696 --> 01:23:31.119
Why would you wait until now?

400
01:23:31.899 --> 01:23:35.023
- Because I don't have any friends.
- What?

401
01:23:35.803 --> 01:23:37.404
What?

402
01:23:40.808 --> 01:23:42.664
"I wanted...

403
01:23:43.044 --> 01:23:46.584
...to show you stuff."

404
01:23:47.681 --> 01:23:50.038
"I wanted to show you stuff"?

405
01:23:50.118 --> 01:23:52.412
- What does that mean?
- I was afraid if you found...

406
01:23:52.492 --> 01:23:57.373
- ...your father, he would take you away.
- What? I can't hear you. I don't understand!

407
01:23:57.453 --> 01:24:00.349
I was going to tell you.

408
01:24:00.729 --> 01:24:04.285
- You were going to tell me?
- Yes, I swear.

409
01:24:04.365 --> 01:24:07.234
How long were you going to take?

410
01:24:10.538 --> 01:24:12.428
I'm sorry, okay?

411
01:24:13.508 --> 01:24:15.041
Please.

412
01:24:16.143 --> 01:24:17.677
Just...

413
01:24:25.519 --> 01:24:27.109
Yeah.

414
01:24:27.389 --> 01:24:31.680
Well, if you wanted to be my friend,
you would've helped me find my father.

415
01:24:31.760 --> 01:24:34.202
But... I'm helping you.

416
01:24:34.282 --> 01:24:37.339
I'm helping you! I'm helping you now!

417
01:25:59.761 --> 01:26:02.169
<i>I turn to the right</i>

418
01:26:03.418 --> 01:26:06.108
<i>A little white light</i>

419
01:26:07.188 --> 01:26:09.878
<i>Will lead you to my</i>

420
01:26:10.258 --> 01:26:13.482
<i>Blue heaven.</i>

421
01:26:14.162 --> 01:26:16.217
- I'll be right with you.
- Hello?

422
01:27:02.109 --> 01:27:05.179
I'm sorry, she's a little high-strung
first thing in the morning.

423
01:27:07.668 --> 01:27:09.206
Hello.

424
01:27:10.918 --> 01:27:13.187
You're scaring off the customers!

425
01:29:05.132 --> 01:29:07.121
Hello, Kincaid's.

426
01:29:07.201 --> 01:29:09.691
Yes, of course. We have lots of Dickens.

427
01:29:10.371 --> 01:29:13.494
Oh, yes, we have four or five
copies, I believe.

428
01:29:13.574 --> 01:29:16.564
Yup. Um, yeah. We're open till 5:00.

429
01:29:16.644 --> 01:29:18.646
Ask for me. Walter.

430
01:29:38.331 --> 01:29:39.975
Hello.

431
01:29:40.055 --> 01:29:42.690
I didn't see you there. Are you...

432
01:29:42.770 --> 01:29:44.604
Did I wake you?

433
01:29:47.874 --> 01:29:49.624
You can't hear?

434
01:29:50.344 --> 01:29:52.145
I don't know sign language.

435
01:29:58.685 --> 01:30:01.175
- You can hear?
- Yes.

436
01:30:01.255 --> 01:30:03.925
- But she's deaf?
- Yes.

437
01:30:08.128 --> 01:30:10.084
I had an accident.

438
01:30:10.164 --> 01:30:13.133
Recently. That's why I can't hear.

439
01:30:20.540 --> 01:30:22.776
You s... you scared us.

440
01:30:29.249 --> 01:30:31.305
Are you okay?

441
01:30:31.385 --> 01:30:33.474
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

442
01:30:33.554 --> 01:30:35.343
Thanks.

443
01:30:35.423 --> 01:30:38.258
I, uh, I just fell asleep for a second.

444
01:31:29.476 --> 01:31:31.510
How do you know my name?

445
01:31:58.722 --> 01:32:00.557
Did you know her?

446
01:32:40.615 --> 01:32:43.049
I came here trying to find my father.

447
01:32:45.018 --> 01:32:47.475
He's looking for his father.

448
01:32:48.155 --> 01:32:50.010
Who are you?

449
01:32:50.090 --> 01:32:51.924
How do you know my name?

450
01:33:12.211 --> 01:33:15.882
I saw you at the wolf diorama in the museum.

451
01:33:20.937 --> 01:33:22.777
What is going on?

452
01:33:23.457 --> 01:33:25.291
Tell me!

453
01:33:43.109 --> 01:33:44.677
Where?

454
01:33:46.479 --> 01:33:47.980
Come.

455
01:36:43.590 --> 01:36:45.125
Whoa.

456
01:36:50.697 --> 01:36:52.265
Wow.

457
01:36:57.604 --> 01:36:59.439
What is this?

458
01:37:01.474 --> 01:37:03.079
Where are we?

459
01:37:30.904 --> 01:37:33.512
"I need you to be patient with this story...

460
01:37:34.352 --> 01:37:36.243
...and read it slowly.

461
01:37:37.377 --> 01:37:41.069
"I've worked in the Queens Museum
of Art for 15 years now...

462
01:37:41.849 --> 01:37:46.314
...but the story I need to tell you,
begins a long time before that.

463
01:37:48.489 --> 01:37:51.346
When I was a little girl in 1927...

464
01:37:52.326 --> 01:37:54.950
<i>...I came to New York
for the very first time.</i>

465
01:37:55.980 --> 01:37:59.467
<i>It was my brother, Walter,
who finally rescued me.</i>

466
01:37:59.900 --> 01:38:03.736
<i>I found him at the Museum of Natural
History, where he was working.</i>

467
01:38:05.339 --> 01:38:09.497
<i>I begged him to help me, to get me
away from my mother and father.</i>

468
01:38:09.577 --> 01:38:14.014
<i>I wanted to stay in New York.
I... I wanted to learn things."</i>

469
01:38:16.549 --> 01:38:18.939
<i>"The first thing Walter did...</i>

470
01:38:19.019 --> 01:38:21.843
<i>...was help me find a school
for deaf children.</i>

471
01:38:23.123 --> 01:38:25.792
<i>I didn't even know such a school existed.</i>

472
01:38:32.365 --> 01:38:34.656
<i>My parents divorced when I was young.</i>

473
01:38:35.336 --> 01:38:39.977
<i>It was a big scandal back then
because my mother was famous.</i>

474
01:38:47.120 --> 01:38:49.804
<i>It was there at school I met my Bill...</i>

475
01:38:49.884 --> 01:38:52.140
<i>...who was training to be a printer.</i>

476
01:38:52.720 --> 01:38:56.395
<i>We married and, before we knew it,
had a baby boy.</i>

477
01:38:58.491 --> 01:39:03.417
<i>Times as they were, everyone worried
about us raising a hearing child...</i>

478
01:39:03.497 --> 01:39:07.500
<i>...but our boy managed to handle
his lack of deafness just fine.</i>

479
01:39:10.169 --> 01:39:12.194
<i>Soon after I'd married...</i>

480
01:39:12.274 --> 01:39:16.276
<i>...Walter helped me get a job at the
museum in the Exhibitions Department.</i>

481
01:39:18.778 --> 01:39:22.648
<i>I'd always loved making models,
so it suited me well.</i>

482
01:39:27.036 --> 01:39:30.662
<i>I worked there for many years
alongside my son.</i>

483
01:39:32.975 --> 01:39:36.097
<i>But soon planning began...</i>

484
01:39:36.177 --> 01:39:39.199
<i>...for the 1964 World's Fair."</i>

485
01:39:41.638 --> 01:39:43.591
<i>The Unisphere would have to withstand...</i>

486
01:39:43.672 --> 01:39:47.862
<i>...the ever-changing forces of nature,
as well as its own enormous weight.</i>

487
01:39:47.942 --> 01:39:51.265
<i>"One of the attractions
was going to be the panorama...</i>

488
01:39:51.345 --> 01:39:55.035
<i>...a scale model
of the entire city of New York...</i>

489
01:39:55.115 --> 01:39:57.772
<i>...the largest architectural
model ever built.</i>

490
01:39:58.452 --> 01:40:03.344
<i>It was an opportunity I couldn't pass up,
so I left the Museum of Natural History...</i>

491
01:40:03.424 --> 01:40:05.881
<i>...to work on the panorama in Queens.</i>

492
01:40:07.561 --> 01:40:10.751
<i>Sadly, my Bill had passed away by then...</i>

493
01:40:10.831 --> 01:40:13.289
<i>...so it was just my son and me.</i>

494
01:40:14.735 --> 01:40:16.588
<i>When the world's fair ended...</i>

495
01:40:16.668 --> 01:40:20.361
<i>...the panorama was so popular,
they decided to keep it open.</i>

496
01:40:20.441 --> 01:40:24.857
<i>But they needed someone to maintain
the model, so they hired me for the job.</i>

497
01:40:26.880 --> 01:40:29.503
<i>Around that time, our son was appointed...</i>

498
01:40:29.583 --> 01:40:34.342
<i>...the lead designer for a new diorama
at the Museum of Natural History...</i>

499
01:40:34.422 --> 01:40:37.945
<i>...the youngest person
ever to have that honor.</i>

500
01:40:38.025 --> 01:40:40.312
<i>But you already know this part.</i>

501
01:40:41.932 --> 01:40:46.729
<i>The job would send him up to Gunflint Lake."</i>

502
01:40:51.704 --> 01:40:53.594
Your son...

503
01:40:54.374 --> 01:40:56.208
...is Danny?

504
01:41:08.755 --> 01:41:10.623
You're my grandmother.

505
01:41:15.562 --> 01:41:17.385
Where is he?

506
01:41:18.215 --> 01:41:21.861
I came all the way
from Minnesota to find him.

507
01:41:23.336 --> 01:41:25.137
Where is he?

508
01:41:56.369 --> 01:42:00.360
"The librarian he'd contacted
to help him with his research...

509
01:42:00.840 --> 01:42:03.294
...happened to own a small cabin...

510
01:42:03.374 --> 01:42:05.411
<i>...and he rented it from her."</i>

511
01:42:07.680 --> 01:42:10.404
Miss Wilson, is that you?
Am I in the right place?

512
01:42:10.484 --> 01:42:14.141
You are indeed, Mr. Lobel.
The cabin is right over there.

513
01:42:14.221 --> 01:42:16.110
It's Danny.

514
01:42:16.590 --> 01:42:19.780
Whoa! I don't like the ice. I don't trust it.

515
01:42:19.860 --> 01:42:23.684
Come on, Danny, it's not
gonna break. It's two feet thick.

516
01:42:23.764 --> 01:42:25.686
- Don't you trust me?
- I'm not sure right now.

517
01:42:25.766 --> 01:42:29.723
<i>"Dear Mom, the research is going well.</i>

518
01:42:29.803 --> 01:42:34.240
<i>Very helpful staff,
especially this librarian."</i>

519
01:42:41.447 --> 01:42:45.707
<i>"Eventually, he completed
the diorama of the wolves.</i>

520
01:42:46.487 --> 01:42:49.343
<i>I still go to visit it whenever I can.</i>

521
01:42:49.423 --> 01:42:52.258
<i>It's the only one he got to make."</i>

522
01:43:24.991 --> 01:43:27.077
"Your dad was ill, Ben.

523
01:43:27.528 --> 01:43:31.719
He had a heart condition,
the same one his father had.

524
01:43:31.799 --> 01:43:34.021
It kept him out of the war...

525
01:43:34.101 --> 01:43:37.624
...but a few years after
he returned from Gunflint Lake...

526
01:43:37.704 --> 01:43:39.538
...his heart..."

527
01:44:42.920 --> 01:44:47.461
"This panorama is not just
a model of New York City.

528
01:44:47.591 --> 01:44:50.826
It's also the story of your father's life.

529
01:44:55.948 --> 01:44:57.723
<i>"When I took the job...</i>

530
01:44:57.803 --> 01:45:01.955
<i>...I thought it would be fun to
secretly personalize the panorama."</i>

531
01:45:04.557 --> 01:45:07.648
<i>"I hid little mementos
from your father's life...</i>

532
01:45:07.728 --> 01:45:09.997
<i>...inside the buildings."</i>

533
01:45:46.179 --> 01:45:48.822
We're gonna miss him.
There's nobody like him.

534
01:45:48.902 --> 01:45:50.257
So, to Danny.

535
01:45:50.337 --> 01:45:52.726
- To Danny.
- Hear, hear.

536
01:45:52.806 --> 01:45:56.764
<i>"I knew everyone at Danny's
funeral except for two people...</i>

537
01:45:56.844 --> 01:45:59.086
<i>...a woman and a little boy.</i>

538
01:45:59.846 --> 01:46:02.299
<i>She introduced herself, and...</i>

539
01:46:02.379 --> 01:46:05.638
<i>...I recognized her name
from Danny's letters.</i>

540
01:46:06.418 --> 01:46:09.509
<i>She told me she'd brought her son earlier...</i>

541
01:46:09.589 --> 01:46:12.479
<i>...and showed him the diorama
Danny had made."</i>

542
01:46:17.531 --> 01:46:19.779
I don't remember any of this.

543
01:46:28.173 --> 01:46:30.330
That's why I dreamed of them.

544
01:46:30.410 --> 01:46:32.231
The wolves.

545
01:46:32.911 --> 01:46:34.779
I saw them.

546
01:46:50.430 --> 01:46:52.269
What happened?

547
01:46:52.899 --> 01:46:55.167
- Help.
- Come.

548
01:47:38.794 --> 01:47:40.784
Jamie?

549
01:47:40.914 --> 01:47:43.137
Wait, how did you get here?

550
01:47:43.217 --> 01:47:45.283
I followed you.

551
01:47:55.361 --> 01:47:56.950
Wait.

552
01:47:57.130 --> 01:47:58.641
- I don't understand.
- What?

553
01:47:58.721 --> 01:48:02.108
- She's the lady from...
- Oh, she...

554
01:48:08.691 --> 01:48:12.165
<i>This special radio red alert news report.</i>

555
01:48:12.245 --> 01:48:17.037
<i>A major power blackout has hit New York City
and surrounding communities.</i>

556
01:48:17.117 --> 01:48:22.042
<i>George, there are individuals
directing traffic at the intersection.</i>

557
01:48:22.122 --> 01:48:24.811
<i>Uh, I assume many of them are policemen.</i>

558
01:49:25.050 --> 01:49:26.585
Oh.

559
01:49:29.922 --> 01:49:31.500
My...

560
01:49:44.653 --> 01:49:45.921
Friend.
